{:edb/db-before-t 2219 :edb/db-after-t 2220 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535532 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:56:08.733Z" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166437 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=7{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166437 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 4x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{1}(t) + 6x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 7x_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = 5x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 2x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186166437 1145 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t)$.\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t)$.\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535532 false] [17592186166437 1145 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\nx'_i(t) = \\lambda_i x_i(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_i$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, $x'_1(t) = 4x_1(t) + 2x_2(t)$ depends on $x_1(t)$ and $x_2(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, $x'_1(t) = -3x_1(t)$ depends on only $x_1(t)$ and $x'_2(t) = 7x_2(t)$ depends on only $x_2(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because, for example, the first equation depends on $x_2(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535532 true] [17592186166438 1100 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-6{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166438 1100 "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations given by\n${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-6x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t)$." 13194139535532 false] [17592186166438 1145 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/17657d7db880fc0ababc24511039a9d7446952f7521e31ae3351adbb6b28ee61.png)\nthe formula for the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/44a286f1a4c15598038f38cdb4140534bac24caacb36f32ee4ef9cbe05b3e5e1.png)\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/d9b9ac7fc20d076110f99476fc48d3c00c4206dc37fb2a929893fcbbb77d6526.png)\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/4f7cfa979200fccf811a7e0d9a633b7919b33b7740c635a921b75ad09995e700.png)" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166438 1145 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\nthe formula for the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write our differential equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ x_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of the differential system is diagonal, the system is decoupled.\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186166439 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14066958/assets/5cc29f7492fded69b4100e10329c831cca0734a29966cd1aac2e8ad017636719.png)" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166439 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\ln (3)\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186166439 1145 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{1t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e^{2\\ln (3)t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}(e^{\\ln (9)})^{t} \\\\\n&= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0)$. Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(0) &= c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{0} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{0} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}c_{1} \\\\ c_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + (-1)\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}e^{t} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}9^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186166439 1145 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{1t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)})}^{t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial value $\\mathbf{x}(0).$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+(-1)[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{9}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186166828 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353932 13194139535532 true] [17592186166828 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353939 13194139535532 true] [17592186166828 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353946 13194139535532 true] [17592186166829 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353911 13194139535532 true] [17592186166829 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353918 13194139535532 true] [17592186166829 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353953 13194139535532 true] [17592186166829 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353960 13194139535532 true] [17592186166829 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353987 13194139535532 true] [17592186166830 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353925 13194139535532 true] [17592186166830 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353967 13194139535532 true] [17592186166830 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353980 13194139535532 true] [17592186166830 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353994 13194139535532 true] [17592186167095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535532 true] [17592186167095 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)$\n- ${x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186167095 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n2. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n3. $x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186167095 1145 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535532 true] [17592186167096 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535532 true] [17592186167096 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186167096 1100 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n1. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) =-x_{2}(t)$\n2. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) =-3x_{1}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = πx_{2}(t)$\n3. ${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) - x_{2}(t)$" 13194139535532 false] [17592186167096 1145 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535532 true] [17592186167254 1017 #edb/ref 17592186165073 13194139535532 false] [17592186167254 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353974 13194139535532 true] [17592186167254 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535532 true] [17592186167254 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186167254 1100 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\ln (2)\\end{bmatrix}x(t), x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535532 false] [17592186167254 1145 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{0} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =5[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{2}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353912 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1089 "q-167507" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1100 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1113 #uuid "5fe31ca3-9692-5c8b-b936-2c7c902b4cf8" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353911 1145 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353913 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353914 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353915 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353916 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353917 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1041 "selection" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353914 13194139535532 true] [17592186353912 1110 #uuid "1abba9be-df64-52b3-8203-fdf82a8a9100" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353913 1034 "x(t)=c[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,c\\in R" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353913 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535532 true] [17592186353913 1111 #uuid "297dfcf0-0119-5f66-a683-25789f86a54d" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353914 1034 "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353914 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535532 true] [17592186353914 1111 #uuid "ab49702a-2642-56d3-aebb-ae2e7031417d" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353915 1034 "x(t)=-6[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{1}t}+6[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{c}_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" 13194139535532 true] [17592186353915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535532 true] [17592186353915 1111 #uuid "6dccdf57-89ca-5905-92d2-f16e9c464c21" 13